## Issue / fix: feat: implement --unbuffered mode for streaming input

## Description
## Summary

Closes #3555.

- Repurpose the existing `--unbuffered`/`-u` flag (previously a POSIX no-op) to enable unbuffered input reading using `fill_buf()`/`consume()` instead of `read_until(b'\n')`
- Partial lines display immediately when piping streaming input like `tail -f` into bat
- Line numbers are auto-disabled in unbuffered mode to avoid confusion with partial lines

## Implementation

- **`src/input.rs`**: Added `read_line_unbuffered()` method to `InputReader` that uses `BufRead::fill_buf()`/`consume()` to return whatever data is available without waiting for a newline. First line still blocks for content type detection.
- **`src/output.rs`**: Added `flush()` to `OutputHandle` so output is flushed after each line in unbuffered mode.
- **`src/controller.rs`**: Wires the unbuffered flag from config to the input reader, and flushes after each printed line.
- **`src/bin/bat/app.rs`** / **`src/bin/bat/clap_app.rs`**: Reads the `--unbuffered` flag, auto-strips `LineNumbers` style component, updated help text.
- **Documentation**: Updated man page, help text snapshots, and all shell completions (bash, fish, zsh, PowerShell).

## Trade-offs

- Syntax highlighting may be imperfect on partial lines (acceptable for streaming use case)
- First line still blocks (needed for content type detection)

