## Issue / fix: Attach image drops in CLI-agent sessions instead of typing the path (drag-and-drop)

## Description
## Description

Fixes #9545.

When a CLI agent (e.g. Claude Code) is the active foreground process in a Warp tab, dragging an image file onto the tab inserts the file path as text into the agent's PTY instead of routing the drop through the same image-paste pipeline that Cmd+V uses. Cmd+V on an image works correctly because `TerminalView::paste` writes the clipboard image bytes to the system clipboard and sends the agent's paste keystroke to the PTY (`0x16` on macOS/Linux, `ESC v` on Windows) — the agent reads its own clipboard and renders `[Image #N]` in its prompt.

This change mirrors that mechanism for drag-and-drop. When a CLI agent session is registered for the terminal, the active block is long-running, and the dropped files are all images:

### Before the fix video:
https://www.loom.com/share/32b03580946249389be828e15e5a7d8d

### After the fix video:
https://www.loom.com/share/b38e4676c5cf4a31ad985bab45fdfdfa

- Each image is read off-thread.
- Bytes are written to the system clipboard as `ImageData`.
- The agent's paste keystroke is sent to the PTY.
- A delay (`CLI_AGENT_IMAGE_PASTE_DELAY`, the existing constant used by `paste_images_then_submit_text`) separates iterations so the agent has time to read the clipboard before it's overwritten.

End user behavior is identical to copying the image to the clipboard and pressing Cmd+V over the agent's TUI.

Mixed drops (some image, some non-image paths) and drops outside CLI-agent sessions fall through 

