## Issue / fix: Improve errors for incomplete math operators and disallow linebreaks in operands

## Description
**Part of the Math Syntax Refactor**
This PR is part of the exhaustive math syntax refactor. It should be considered exploratory, and not necessarily a final decision, even if merged to the `math-syntax` branch. Non-specific feedback for this change should go in one of its related issues.

---

This PR tightens up our handling of general operators in math (primes will be dealt with separately).

Closes #1435

### First commit
In this commit, we now error more predictably when an operator has a missing side, and the error message we give is much better. This resolves the odd behavior of `$(1/)$` parsing the close-paren as the fraction's denominator, now giving the error, "expected an expression to the right of the operator".

I implemented this by making better use of the `stop_set` argument in `math_expr_prec`, ensuring that we thread it through recursive operator calls when parsing an operator's right operand, and erroring if we start the call at the stop set.

This also improves the error for unclosed delimiters used for grouping. So `$($` alone is still fine, but `$1/($` is an error. This was done by passing in the precedence of the surrounding expression to `math_delimited` and checking that it was higher than zero if we didn't find a closing delimiter.

And I removed the `MATH_EXPR` syntax set because the `unexpected` error message case was really only used for these operators, and I would prefer to give a more explicit error for them.

