## Issue / fix: fix(rust): Rebalance deep merge_sorted chains

## Description
Fixes #27051 

As a follow up from https://github.com/pola-rs/polars/issues/26960#issuecomment-4117256897, deep `merge_sorted` chains were ending in a very left-deep optimized plan. For small cases this is mostly ok, but for longer chains it makes the plan too deep, hurts paralellism, and can blow up in places that still walk the tree recursively. This change adds a small optimizer pass that collects adjacent MergeSorted nodes with the same key and rebuilds them into a balanced binary tree before `CHECK_ORDER_OBSERVE` can lower them to Union. It keeps the same leaf order and does not cross key boundaries.

I made 2 tests in `optimization_checks.rs` covering the basics but I can add more if you think we should cover more cases

I also checked the plan shape manualy. Before the fix an 8-input chained merge_sorted("a") was still left-deep even after optimization:

```
  merge(a)
  └─ merge(a)
     └─ merge(a)
        └─ merge(a)
           └─ merge(a)
              └─ merge(a)
                 └─ merge(a)
                    ├─ df
                    └─ df
```

After the fix, the unoptimized plan is still left-deep, but the optimized one gets rebalanced into a proper binary tree:

```
  merge(a)
  ├─ merge(a)
  │  ├─ merge(a)
  │  │  ├─ df
  │  │  └─ df
  │  └─ merge(a)
  │     ├─ df
  │     └─ df
  └─ merge(a)
     ├─ merge(a)
     │  ├─ df
     │  └─ df
     └─ merge(a)
        ├─ df
        └─ df
```

I didn’t do a pure flatten pass bec

